    "an.hidden.deep": "Unicode Tag characters (U+E0000 to U+E007F), zero-width characters and bidirectional overrides show up as nothing in editors, but the model reads them as text. Attackers use them for indirect prompt injection in rules files, skills and READMEs. AgathOS counts these characters, decodes Tag text back to readable letters and flags long zero-width runs, which point to hidden encoded data.",
    "an.inject.deep": "Indirect prompt injection: text the agent reads as data is followed as instructions, because a model has no hard line between the two. AgathOS matches known override phrases and data-theft commands (a download piped into a shell, webhook addresses) after decoding hidden characters. A match proves the intent of the text, not that an agent has already acted on it.",
    "an.secret.deep": "AgathOS recognises provider key formats (for example sk-ant-, sk-proj-, xai-, gsk_, ghp_, AKIA) and provider variable names, and shows them masked. Agents, MCP servers and extensions that read the folder can read the key, and one injected instruction can send it out. A key in a repository's history stays valid until you revoke it.",
    "an.mcpBad.deep": "The package name and version match a public report of a malicious or hijacked MCP server (for example postmark-mcp, which secretly copied every email to the attacker). An MCP server runs as a local program with your rights and receives the arguments of every tool call, so it sees exactly what the agent handles.",
    "an.supply.deep": "Launchers such as npx -y or uvx without a version fetch the newest release from the registry at every start. A hijacked maintainer account or a look-alike package name is then enough to run new code without any change on your side, as in the Shai-Hulud and s1ngularity attacks. AgathOS also flags servers started from temp folders, shell pipes in the definition and definitions that changed after you approved them.",
    "an.remote.deep": "Remote MCP servers (HTTP or SSE) run on machines you do not control. The tool descriptions they send become part of the prompt, so whoever runs the server can change the agent's behaviour at any time (tool poisoning, rug pull). Plain http lets anyone on the network path read and change the traffic.",
    "an.hijack.deep": "Agent settings decide which tools, hooks and MCP servers an agent has. An agent or package that edits these files can give itself new tools or a hook that runs in every session, which is a step to stay and gain power. AgathOS pins approved definitions by hash and reports every change, including which agent was running at that moment.",
    "an.approve.deep": "Auto-approve modes (bypassPermissions, --dangerously-skip-permissions, --yolo, approval_policy = never, chat.tools.autoApprove) remove the human check between the model's decision and its execution. A prompt injection then goes straight to shell commands and file writes. AgathOS reads these settings from config files and command lines.",
    "an.baseUrl.deep": "Variables such as ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL or OPENAI_BASE_URL send all API traffic to another address. A host that is not the provider's official endpoint receives your prompts, your files and the API key in every request. AgathOS checks environment variables, shell startup files and configs against the official hosts.",
    "an.model.deep": "PyTorch .pt, .bin and .ckpt files are Python pickles: loading them runs the instructions inside, and the GLOBAL and REDUCE opcodes can call any function, such as os.system or subprocess. AgathOS walks the pickle opcodes without running them and lists every function it imports; anything outside normal tensor building is reported. safetensors and GGUF files contain no code.",
    "an.port.deep": "The server listens on 0.0.0.0 or ::, so it accepts connections from every network. Ollama, LM Studio and similar servers have no login by default: anyone who can reach the port can run prompts, download or delete models and use your graphics card. Tens of thousands of such servers can be reached from the internet.",
    "an.destroy.deep": "AgathOS counts deleted files per folder within ten seconds and recognises commands such as rm -rf, rmdir /s, Remove-Item -Recurse, del /s and git clean -fdx. Mass deletion by an agent usually comes from a wrong path or a too-broad clean-up instruction, and sometimes from deliberate sabotage. Above the limit AgathOS freezes the running agents.",
    "an.persist.deep": "Scheduled tasks, Run keys, startup items and LaunchAgents start code at every start or login without anyone starting it. AgathOS flags entries that run hidden or encoded scripts (powershell -enc, mshta, osascript, curl piped into sh), run from temp or Downloads folders, or use script runtimes such as node or python.",
    "an.cred.deep": "AgathOS matches file paths and commands against places where credentials live: SSH keys, cloud credentials, .npmrc and .pypirc, browser profiles and cookies, wallets, password managers, the Windows credential store and the macOS keychain. Reading them is the collecting step before data theft.",
    "an.secOff.deep": "Commands that switch off Defender, change firewall rules or stop security services remove the visibility that later steps would trigger. With AgathOS Guard on Windows, new firewall rules from other programs are reported together with the program that made them.",
    "an.exfil.deep": "The lethal trifecta (Simon Willison): an agent that reads untrusted content, has access to private data and can communicate outside can be steered by that content to send the private data away. AgathOS tracks these three labels per session from the agent's actions and raises the alarm when all three are present, or when data goes to an address that is not a known AI or developer service.",
    "an.chain.deep": "AgathOS keeps per agent how often one kind of action follows another, and compares the recent steps with attack templates (stealing secrets, data theft, takeover, destruction, planting). It warns when only the last step of a template is missing, before that step happens. A prediction is a probability, not a fact.",
    "an.odd.deep": "Every agent gets a baseline of action types and transitions, learned only from clean or approved sessions and never from sessions that read untrusted content. The score rises when the current actions are rare for this agent on this computer. Approving a session teaches AgathOS that the behaviour is normal.",
    "an.honesty.deep": "AgathOS compares the agent's transcript (Claude Code, Codex) with what the operating system actually saw: claims such as 'all tests pass' without a test run or after a failed one, changes to test files during a bug fix, and risky commands that never appear in the conversation.",
    "an.tamper.deep": "Every log line contains the SHA-256 hash of the line before it. Changing or removing a line breaks the chain from that point, which AgathOS checks at start and when you ask. A break means the file was edited outside AgathOS.",
    "an.generic.deep": "A rule of thumb was triggered. The evidence above shows which signal it was.",
