    "link.liveLookalike": "{agent} connects to {host}, which looks like {brand} but is not",
    "link.liveTunnel": "{agent} connects to {host}, a tunnel to somebody else’s computer",
    "link.liveCatchall": "{agent} sends data to {host}, a site that only collects it",
    "an.linkBad.simple": "A program is set to send your AI prompts, and maybe your key, to an address that is not the real provider. Whoever runs that address can read everything and change the answers.",
    "an.linkBad.deep": "AgathOS reads the base address of every AI program: settings files, variables and project folders. This address points to a name that imitates a provider, a tunnel to somebody else’s computer, a site that only collects data, or an address that changed after you approved it. The program then sends prompts, the files it reads and its key there, and the other side can reply with instructions of its own (an attacker in the middle).",
    "an.linkBad.maybe": "A tunnel can be your own test setup, and an address can change on purpose. A project from a stranger that brings its own address almost never is.",
    "an.linkBad.risk": "The key can be stolen, private code can leak, and a hostile server can steer the agent with forged answers.",
    "an.linkBad.todo": "Open the file shown, put the official address back or remove the line, then replace the key if it was used. Only choose “This is OK” if you know and trust the address.",
    "an.linkOdd.simple": "A program sends prompts to an address that AgathOS does not know as an AI provider, or that is not encrypted or is open to other devices.",
    "an.linkOdd.deep": "Companies and hobbyists do use their own gateways, proxies and servers on the home network. From the name alone AgathOS cannot tell those from a stranger’s server, so it asks you once. After you approve, it only speaks up if the address changes.",
    "an.linkOdd.maybe": "Most of the time this is your own gateway, a hosting platform you chose, or a server on your network.",
    "an.linkOdd.risk": "A server you do not know can read the prompts and the key; on an unencrypted link anyone on the same network can read them too.",
    "an.linkOdd.todo": "If you set this up yourself, choose “This is OK”. If not, find out who added it and put the official address back.",
    "an.linkAgent.simple": "One AI agent starts or calls another one. That second agent has its own rights and its own key.",
    "an.linkAgent.deep": "Agents can start each other (a CLI starting another CLI, or an MCP server that is itself an agent). Every step passes on work and trust, and a hidden instruction in a file can travel along the chain. AgathOS notes who started whom and asks you once.",
    "an.linkAgent.maybe": "Many people let one agent ask another for a second opinion, on purpose.",
    "an.linkAgent.risk": "A planted instruction can use a trusted agent to reach a second agent with more rights, so the damage is harder to trace.",
    "an.linkAgent.todo": "If you wanted this, choose “This is OK”. If not, stop the agent that started it and read what it was asked to do.",

