    "board.moved": "{app}: the message board {name} changed ({label})",
    "fix.board": "Check who can write on this board. If you do not know it, remove the line that names it and look at what the agent was asked to do. If you set it up yourself, choose “This is OK”: AgathOS then warns you when it changes.",
    "an.boardBad.simple": "An agent is connected to a place where strangers can leave messages, or the address hides something. Whatever is written there can steer the agent.",
    "an.boardBad.deep": "AgathOS searches the files and settings that agents read for message boards: chat channels, public channels and text pages, hidden networks and networks of agents. This one is open to anyone, accepts messages from anyone, hides invisible characters in its address, is a hidden address (Tor or I2P), or changed after you approved it. An agent that reads it treats the text as input, and a planted instruction can make it act or send data away (indirect prompt injection).",
    "an.boardBad.maybe": "A public channel can be a test setup or a bot you built yourself. A channel that accepts messages from everybody, or an address with invisible characters, almost never is.",
    "an.boardBad.risk": "A stranger can give the agent orders, or collect what the agent sends there: files, keys and notes.",
    "an.boardBad.todo": "Remove the line that names this board, or close the channel to strangers, and replace any key that stood there. Only choose “This is OK” if you know the place and trust everybody who can write there.",
    "an.boardOrders.simple": "A file tells an agent to fetch instructions from an address and follow them, once or again and again. Whoever owns that address can then give the agent orders.",
    "an.boardOrders.deep": "Some skills and instruction files make an agent check a page or a network “every few hours” and do what it finds: a hidden message board, also called a dead drop. The owner can change that text at any moment, so the agent follows orders that were never in the file you reviewed. AgathOS recognises this pattern in many languages: fetch, follow, and “every N hours”.",
    "an.boardOrders.maybe": "Some tools really do read a task list from a page you own. If the address is yours, this is fine.",
    "an.boardOrders.risk": "The owner can steer the agent long after you checked the file, for example to read private files or to send them away.",
    "an.boardOrders.todo": "Read the file that names it. If you do not know this address, remove those lines. If it is your own page, choose “This is OK”: AgathOS then warns you when the file points somewhere else.",
    "an.boardOdd.simple": "An agent can talk to a chat, a webhook or a public channel. That is a way in for orders and a way out for data.",
    "an.boardOdd.deep": "Chat bots, outgoing webhooks, message channels and networks of agents are normal tools, and also the paths that attacks on agents use: orders come in, data goes out. AgathOS lists each one it finds in files, settings and MCP servers, and, with Guard, in the connections that programs make while you work. It asks you once per board.",
    "an.boardOdd.maybe": "Most of the time this is a bot or a notification you set up yourself.",
    "an.boardOdd.risk": "If a stranger can write there, they can steer the agent. If the address belongs to a stranger, everything sent there is theirs.",
    "an.boardOdd.todo": "If you set this up yourself, choose “This is OK”. If not, find out who added it and remove it.",
    "set.boardCheck": "Message boards: look for places where agents read orders or leave data",
    "set.boardCheckDesc": "Searches instruction files, skills and settings for chat channels, webhooks, public text pages and networks of agents, including files that tell an agent to fetch and follow orders. Nothing is sent anywhere and keys are never shown.",

